      "id": 1,
      "text": "Van Hornesville sits in the town of Stark, north of Springfield Center, along NY 80 in Herkimer County, New York.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Van_Hornesville%2C_New_York",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Van Hornesville sprang to life after the American Revolution, thanks to Otsquago Creek and Abraham Van Horne’s vision.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.vhccorp.org/our-community",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Abraham Van Horne, a Quartermaster in the Tryon County Militia, saw Otsquago’s waterpower and planned a mill.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.vhccorp.org/our-community",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Abraham Van Horne returned with two sons in 1791, and by 1793 a mill was running on the creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.vhccorp.org/our-community",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Owen D. Young served as Van Hornesville’s guardian angel during the twentieth century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.vhccorp.org/our-community",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Owen D. Young also acted as a major patron of Van Hornesville, supporting local initiatives.",
